  'business-roi': {"title":"Business ROI: Revenue & Total Cost","metaDesc":"Free Business ROI: enter the Revenue and Total Cost and get a roi of 33.33%. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/business/business-roi","cat":"business","catName":"Business","lsi":["marketing roi calculator with campaign cost","return on investment","business roi formula","how to calculate business roi","roi formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Business ROI do?</h2>\n<p>Business ROI works out the roi from the Revenue and Total Cost, following standard Business conventions — the page defaults produce a roi of 33.33%.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Revenue and Total Cost.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the roi, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Business formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (revenue of 100,000, total cost of 75,000), business roi returns a roi of 33.33%. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Business ROI work?</h2>\n<p>Business ROI computes the roi directly from your inputs — the Revenue and Total Cost feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Business ROI works</h2>\n<p>Business ROI turns the values you enter into a verified roi — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it.</p>\n<h2>Using the Business ROI</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Revenue</strong> — the value business roi feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Total Cost</strong> — a core input business roi applies in the formula — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>Check the result. The roi is shown as soon as the inputs are valid, and the steps beneath it show exactly how business roi derived it.</li>\n<li>Explore. Each input change in business roi recalculates instantly; watching the roi move tells you which factor dominates your case.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Business ROI substitutes the Revenue and Total Cost into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the roi rounded for readability.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with revenue of 100,000, total cost of 75,000, business roi returns <strong>ROI: 33.33%</strong>. The same run reports Profit: $25000.00.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: ROI = (Revenue - Cost) / Cost × 100</li><li>Profit = $100000 - $75000 = $25000.00</li><li>ROI = $25000.00 / $75000 × 100 = 33.33%</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The result panel in Business ROI leads with the roi and follows with intermediate values; if the headline surprises you, the steps usually reveal which input is responsible.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Business ROI: business roi planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the roi. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Business ROI is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the roi. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> The result is shareable — the link carries your inputs, so a colleague can open the same calculation in Business ROI without retyping it.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Treat the roi from Business ROI as a planning figure rather than a binding quote, and confirm important decisions with the relevant professional.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the roi arrives with supporting figures and a full step list, Business ROI gives you context rather than a single bare number.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Business ROI calculate?","a":"Business ROI answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the roi? Enter the Revenue and Total Cost, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath. Because Business ROI works the same on phone and desktop, keeps working offline after the first visit, and never asks for sign-up."},{"q":"How is the roi calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: roi = (revenue - cost) / cost × 100, then profit = $100000 - $75000 = $25000.00. The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Business ROI makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the roi is hidden."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Business ROI?","a":"The Revenue and Total Cost that business roi asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the roi instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Business ROI mean?","a":"The main number business roi returns is the roi for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Very large or very small inputs can push the roi from Business ROI beyond what is practically meaningful — sanity-check extreme values before relying on them."},{"q":"When is the Business ROI most useful?","a":"Students, planners, and professionals use business roi for business roi planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the roi, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else. Bookmark business roi — after the first visit it works offline, so the roi is one tap away even without a connection."}]},
