  'stair-stringer': {"title":"Stair Stringer Calculator: Total Rise, Number of Risers","metaDesc":"Free Stair Stringer Calculator: enter the Total Rise and Number of Risers and get a riser height of 7.500 in. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/construction/stair-stringer","cat":"construction","catName":"Construction","lsi":["stair stringer calculator riser height tread depth total rise run building code","stair stringer formula","how to calculate stair stringer","riser height formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Stair Stringer Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Stair Stringer Calculator works out the riser height from the Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth, following standard Construction conventions — the page defaults produce a riser height of 7.500 in.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the riser height, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Construction formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (total rise of 105, number of risers of 14, tread depth of 10), stair stringer calculator returns a riser height of 7.500 in. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Stair Stringer Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Stair Stringer Calculator computes the riser height directly from your inputs — the Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Stair Stringer Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Every run of Stair Stringer Calculator evaluates the Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth you enter, applies the standard Construction formula, and reports the riser height with each step listed for review.</p>\n<h2>Using the Stair Stringer Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Total Rise</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in stair stringer calculator, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Number of Risers</strong> — the value stair stringer calculator feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Tread Depth</strong> — a core input stair stringer calculator applies in the formula — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>Read the result. The riser height appears immediately in stair stringer calculator, with the step-by-step working underneath so you can verify every number.</li>\n<li>Compare scenarios. Rerun with different values, or use the batch mode, to line up several outcomes side by side in stair stringer calculator.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The formula operates on the values exactly as entered; keeping the units shown in each label is what makes the riser height from Stair Stringer Calculator trustworthy.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with total rise of 105, number of risers of 14, tread depth of 10, stair stringer calculator returns <strong>Riser Height: 7.500 in</strong>. The same run reports Total run: 130.0 in (10.83 ft) | Comfort rule 2r+t = 25.0 in (aim 24–25) | Most codes cap riser at 7¾ in.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: riser height = total rise ÷ number of risers</li><li>105 in ÷ 14 = 7.500 in per riser</li><li>Total run = tread × (risers − 1) = 10 × 13 = 130.0 in</li><li>Check 2r + t = 25.0 in — between 24 and 25 in feels comfortable</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Read the riser height first, then the steps: together they show not just the value but why that value follows from your inputs in Stair Stringer Calculator.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Students, planners, and professionals use stair stringer calculator for stair stringer planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the riser height, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Stair Stringer Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the riser height. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Stair Stringer Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the riser height is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Treat the riser height from Stair Stringer Calculator as a planning figure rather than a binding quote, and confirm important decisions with the relevant professional.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Stair Stringer Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Stair Stringer Calculator calculate?","a":"At its core, Stair Stringer Calculator takes the Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth and evaluates the standard formula step by step, so the riser height can be checked rather than trusted on faith. Because the working is visible: Stair Stringer Calculator shows each operation behind the riser height in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the riser height calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: riser height = total rise ÷ number of risers, then 105 in ÷ 14 = 7.500 in per riser. Stair Stringer Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the riser height can be checked line by line."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Stair Stringer Calculator?","a":"The Total Rise, Number of Risers, and Tread Depth that stair stringer calculator asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the riser height instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Stair Stringer Calculator mean?","a":"The main number stair stringer calculator returns is the riser height for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Very large or very small inputs can push the riser height from Stair Stringer Calculator beyond what is practically meaningful — sanity-check extreme values before relying on them."},{"q":"When is the Stair Stringer Calculator most useful?","a":"Stair Stringer Calculator fits planning and checking: stair stringer planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the riser height, or any moment when the riser height needs to be right the first time. If the riser height looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number in Stair Stringer Calculator departed from expectation."}]},
