  'heat-exchanger': {"title":"Heat Exchanger (LMTD): Hot In, Hot Out & Cold In","metaDesc":"Free Heat Exchanger (LMTD): enter the Hot In and Hot Out and get a log mean temperature of LMTD = 40.0°C. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/engineering/heat-exchanger","cat":"engineering","catName":"Engineering","lsi":["heat exchanger calculator lmtd approach temperature","heat exchanger lmtd formula","how to calculate heat exchanger","log mean temperature formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) do?</h2>\n<p>Heat Exchanger (LMTD) works out the log mean temperature from the Hot In, Hot Out, and Cold In, following standard Engineering conventions — the page defaults produce a log mean temperature of LMTD = 40.0°C.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Hot In, Hot Out, and Cold In.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the log mean temperature, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Engineering formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (hot in of 90, hot out of 60, cold in of 20), heat exchanger (lmtd) returns a log mean temperature of LMTD = 40.0°C. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) work?</h2>\n<p>Heat Exchanger (LMTD) computes the log mean temperature directly from your inputs — the Hot In, Hot Out, and Cold In feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) works</h2>\n<p>Heat Exchanger (LMTD) keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Hot In, Hot Out, and Cold In, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line.</p>\n<h2>Using the Heat Exchanger (LMTD)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hot In</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in heat exchanger (lmtd), so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot Out</strong> — the value heat exchanger (lmtd) feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Cold In</strong> — a core input heat exchanger (lmtd) applies in the formula — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>Read the result. The log mean temperature appears immediately in heat exchanger (lmtd), with the step-by-step working underneath so you can verify every number.</li>\n<li>Compare scenarios. Rerun with different values, or use the batch mode, to line up several outcomes side by side in heat exchanger (lmtd).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The calculation in Heat Exchanger (LMTD) applies the standard Engineering method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with hot in of 90, hot out of 60, cold in of 20, heat exchanger (lmtd) returns <strong>LMTD = 40.0°C</strong>. The same run reports ΔT₁=40°C ΔT₂=40°C.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>ΔT₁ = 90 − 50 = 40°C</li><li>ΔT₂ = 60 − 20 = 40°C</li><li>LMTD = (ΔT₁−ΔT₂)/ln(ΔT₁/ΔT₂) = 40.0°C</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Read the log mean temperature first, then the steps: together they show not just the value but why that value follows from your inputs in Heat Exchanger (LMTD).</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Students, planners, and professionals use heat exchanger (lmtd) for heat exchanger lmtd planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the log mean temperature, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic heat exchanger (lmtd) mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Heat Exchanger (LMTD) twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the log mean temperature is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The log mean temperature is only as complete as the inputs: anything Heat Exchanger (LMTD) does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because comparing scenarios takes seconds: change one input at a time in Heat Exchanger (LMTD) and watch the log mean temperature move, which is the fastest way to understand what drives it.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) calculate?","a":"Use Heat Exchanger (LMTD) when the log mean temperature needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Engineering method and shows the working, not just the answer. Because the page doubles as documentation: Heat Exchanger (LMTD) puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the log mean temperature calculated?","a":"The first steps are δt₁ = 90 − 50 = 40°c, then δt₂ = 60 − 20 = 40°c. The engine behind Heat Exchanger (LMTD) evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the log mean temperature you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Heat Exchanger (LMTD)?","a":"The Hot In, Hot Out, and Cold In that heat exchanger (lmtd) asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the log mean temperature instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) mean?","a":"The main number heat exchanger (lmtd) returns is the log mean temperature for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Results from Heat Exchanger (LMTD) are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply."},{"q":"When is the Heat Exchanger (LMTD) most useful?","a":"Heat Exchanger (LMTD) fits planning and checking: heat exchanger lmtd planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the log mean temperature, or any moment when the log mean temperature needs to be right the first time. If the log mean temperature looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number in Heat Exchanger (LMTD) departed from expectation."}]},
