  'capital-gains': {"title":"Capital Gains Tax: Purchase Price, Sale Price & Tax Rate","metaDesc":"Free Capital Gains Tax: enter the Purchase Price and Sale Price and get a tax of $750.00. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/finance/capital-gains","cat":"finance","catName":"Finance","lsi":["capital gains tax","capital gains tax formula","how to calculate capital gains","tax formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Capital Gains Tax do?</h2>\n<p>Capital Gains Tax works out the tax from the Purchase Price, Sale Price, and Tax Rate, following standard Finance conventions — the page defaults produce a tax of $750.00.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Purchase Price, Sale Price, and Tax Rate.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the tax, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Finance formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (purchase price of 10,000, sale price of 15,000, tax rate of 15), capital gains tax returns a tax of $750.00. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Capital Gains Tax work?</h2>\n<p>Capital Gains Tax computes the tax directly from your inputs — the Purchase Price, Sale Price, and Tax Rate feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Capital Gains Tax works</h2>\n<p>This page is a working capital gains tax: enter your values, read the tax, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived.</p>\n<h2>Using the Capital Gains Tax</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Purchase Price</strong> — one of the values capital gains tax builds the calculation from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Sale Price</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in capital gains tax, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Tax Rate</strong> — the value capital gains tax feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>Note the tax. It updates as you type, and the worked steps below it in capital gains tax make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>Adjust and re-run. Change one input at a time to see how sensitive the tax is to it — the fastest way to understand what capital gains tax is doing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rounding in Capital Gains Tax follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the tax is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with purchase price of 10,000, sale price of 15,000, tax rate of 15, capital gains tax returns <strong>Tax: $750.00</strong>. The same run reports Gain: $5000.00.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Gain = $15000 - $10000 = $5000.00</li><li>Tax = 15% × $5000.00 = $750.00</li><li>Net gain = $4250.00</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The tax is the headline answer; the supporting figures beneath it and the step list in Capital Gains Tax give the surrounding context needed to judge it.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Capital Gains Tax fits planning and checking: capital gains tax planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the tax, or any moment when the tax needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Copying the tax without its assumptions is the frequent error — the number from Capital Gains Tax is valid for exactly the inputs shown, so carry the context with it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Bookmark capital gains tax — after the first visit it works offline, so the tax is one tap away even without a connection.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Inputs outside a reasonable range may produce a tax that is mathematically correct but practically implausible; the steps panel in Capital Gains Tax helps you spot that quickly.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the tax arrives with supporting figures and a full step list, Capital Gains Tax gives you context rather than a single bare number.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Capital Gains Tax calculate?","a":"Capital Gains Tax turns the values you enter into a verified tax — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because Capital Gains Tax works the same on phone and desktop, keeps working offline after the first visit, and never asks for sign-up."},{"q":"How is the tax calculated?","a":"The first steps are gain = $15000 - $10000 = $5000.00, then tax = 15% × $5000.00 = $750.00. Capital Gains Tax substitutes the Purchase Price, Sale Price, and Tax Rate into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the tax rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Capital Gains Tax?","a":"The Purchase Price, Sale Price, and Tax Rate that capital gains tax asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the tax instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Capital Gains Tax mean?","a":"The main number capital gains tax returns is the tax for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Capital Gains Tax assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the tax proportionally."},{"q":"When is the Capital Gains Tax most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Capital Gains Tax include capital gains tax planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the tax — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. The result is shareable — the link carries your inputs, so a colleague can open the same calculation in Capital Gains Tax without retyping it."}]},
