  'rental-yield': {"title":"Rental Yield Calculator: Property Price, Monthly Rent","metaDesc":"Free Rental Yield Calculator: enter the Property Price and Monthly Rent and get a net yield of 7.50%. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/finance/rental-yield","cat":"finance","catName":"Finance","lsi":["rental yield calculator","rental yield formula","how to calculate rental yield","net yield formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Rental Yield Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Rental Yield Calculator works out the net yield from the Property Price, Monthly Rent, and Annual Costs, following standard Finance conventions — the page defaults produce a net yield of 7.50%.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Property Price, Monthly Rent, and Annual Costs.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the net yield, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Finance formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (property price of 200,000, monthly rent of 1,500, annual costs of 3,000), rental yield calculator returns a net yield of 7.50%. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Rental Yield Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Rental Yield Calculator computes the net yield directly from your inputs — the Property Price, Monthly Rent, and Annual Costs feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Rental Yield Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Use Rental Yield Calculator when the net yield needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Finance method and shows the working, not just the answer.</p>\n<h2>Using the Rental Yield Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Property Price</strong> — a core input rental yield calculator applies in the formula — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li><strong>Monthly Rent</strong> — one of the values rental yield calculator builds the calculation from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual Costs</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in rental yield calculator, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Review the output. Beyond the headline net yield, rental yield calculator lists the intermediate steps — useful for catching a mistyped input.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the net yield shows which lever matters most for your rental yield question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The engine behind Rental Yield Calculator evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the net yield you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with property price of 200,000, monthly rent of 1,500, annual costs of 3,000, rental yield calculator returns <strong>Net Yield: 7.50%</strong>. The same run reports Gross Yield: 9.00%.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Annual rent = $1500×12 = $18000.00</li><li>Net income = $18000.00 - $3000 = $15000.00</li><li>Net yield = $15000.00 / $200000 × 100 = 7.50%</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Interpret the net yield against the inputs that produced it — the same number from different inputs can mean different things, which is why Rental Yield Calculator always shows the pairing.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Rental Yield Calculator include rental yield planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the net yield — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic rental yield mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> If the net yield looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number in Rental Yield Calculator departed from expectation.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Rental Yield Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the net yield proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because comparing scenarios takes seconds: change one input at a time in Rental Yield Calculator and watch the net yield move, which is the fastest way to understand what drives it.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Rental Yield Calculator calculate?","a":"Every run of Rental Yield Calculator evaluates the Property Price, Monthly Rent, and Annual Costs you enter, applies the standard Finance formula, and reports the net yield with each step listed for review. Because the page doubles as documentation: Rental Yield Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the net yield calculated?","a":"The first steps are annual rent = $1500×12 = $18000.00, then net income = $18000.00 - $3000 = $15000.00. The formula operates on the values exactly as entered; keeping the units shown in each label is what makes the net yield from Rental Yield Calculator trustworthy."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Rental Yield Calculator?","a":"The Property Price, Monthly Rent, and Annual Costs that rental yield calculator asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the net yield instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Rental Yield Calculator mean?","a":"The main number rental yield calculator returns is the net yield for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Rental Yield Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the Rental Yield Calculator most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for Rental Yield Calculator: rental yield planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the net yield. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers. Run Rental Yield Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the net yield is, which a single run never shows."}]},
