  'black-scholes': {"title":"Black-Scholes Option Pricing: Spot Price, Strike Price","metaDesc":"Free Black-Scholes Option Pricing: enter the Spot Price and Strike Price and get a call of $4.58 | Put: $6.99. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/finance/black-scholes","cat":"finance","catName":"Finance","lsi":["black scholes option pricing calculator","black scholes option formula","how to calculate black scholes","call formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Black-Scholes Option Pricing do?</h2>\n<p>Black-Scholes Option Pricing works out the call from the Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate, following standard Finance conventions — the page defaults produce a call of $4.58 | Put: $6.99.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the call, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Finance formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (spot price of 100, strike price of 105, risk-free rate of 5), black-scholes option pricing returns a call of $4.58 | Put: $6.99. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Black-Scholes Option Pricing work?</h2>\n<p>Black-Scholes Option Pricing computes the call directly from your inputs — the Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Black-Scholes Option Pricing works</h2>\n<p>Black-Scholes Option Pricing turns the values you enter into a verified call — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it.</p>\n<h2>Using the Black-Scholes Option Pricing</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Spot Price</strong> — the value black-scholes option pricing feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Strike Price</strong> — a core input black-scholes option pricing applies in the formula — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk-Free Rate</strong> — one of the values black-scholes option pricing builds the calculation from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li>Check the result. The call is shown as soon as the inputs are valid, and the steps beneath it show exactly how black-scholes option pricing derived it.</li>\n<li>Explore. Each input change in black-scholes option pricing recalculates instantly; watching the call move tells you which factor dominates your case.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Black-Scholes Option Pricing substitutes the Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the call rounded for readability.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with spot price of 100, strike price of 105, risk-free rate of 5, black-scholes option pricing returns <strong>Call: $4.58 | Put: $6.99</strong>. The same run reports d₁=-0.0975 | d₂=-0.2389.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>d₁ = (ln(S/K) + (r+σ²/2)T) / (σ√T)</li><li>d₂ = d₁ − σ√T</li><li>Call = S·N(d₁) − K·e^(-rT)·N(d₂)</li><li>Put = K·e^(-rT)·N(−d₂) − S·N(−d₁)</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The result panel in Black-Scholes Option Pricing leads with the call and follows with intermediate values; if the headline surprises you, the steps usually reveal which input is responsible.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Black-Scholes Option Pricing: black scholes option planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the call. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic black-scholes option pricing mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> The result is shareable — the link carries your inputs, so a colleague can open the same calculation in Black-Scholes Option Pricing without retyping it.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The model behind Black-Scholes Option Pricing covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Black-Scholes Option Pricing runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Black-Scholes Option Pricing calculate?","a":"Black-Scholes Option Pricing answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the call? Enter the Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath. Because the call arrives with supporting figures and a full step list, Black-Scholes Option Pricing gives you context rather than a single bare number."},{"q":"How is the call calculated?","a":"The first steps are d₁ = (ln(s/k) + (r+σ²/2)t) / (σ√t), then d₂ = d₁ − σ√t. The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Black-Scholes Option Pricing makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the call is hidden."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Black-Scholes Option Pricing?","a":"The Spot Price, Strike Price, and Risk-Free Rate that black-scholes option pricing asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the call instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Black-Scholes Option Pricing mean?","a":"The main number black-scholes option pricing returns is the call for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The call is only as complete as the inputs: anything Black-Scholes Option Pricing does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation."},{"q":"When is the Black-Scholes Option Pricing most useful?","a":"Students, planners, and professionals use black-scholes option pricing for black scholes option planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the call, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else. Bookmark black-scholes option pricing — after the first visit it works offline, so the call is one tap away even without a connection."}]},
