  'pendulum': {"title":"Pendulum Period: Length & Gravity","metaDesc":"Free Pendulum Period: enter the Length and Gravity and get a period of 2.006 s. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/science/pendulum","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["pendulum period calculator simple length gravity","pendulum period formula","how to calculate pendulum period","period formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Pendulum Period do?</h2>\n<p>Pendulum Period works out the period from the Length and Gravity, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a period of 2.006 s.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Length and Gravity.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the period, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (length of 1, gravity of 9.81), pendulum period returns a period of 2.006 s. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Pendulum Period work?</h2>\n<p>Pendulum Period computes the period directly from your inputs — the Length and Gravity feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Pendulum Period works</h2>\n<p>Pendulum Period keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Length and Gravity, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line.</p>\n<h2>Using the Pendulum Period</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Length</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in pendulum period, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity</strong> — the value pendulum period feeds directly into its formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>Read the result. The period appears immediately in pendulum period, with the step-by-step working underneath so you can verify every number.</li>\n<li>Compare scenarios. Rerun with different values, or use the batch mode, to line up several outcomes side by side in pendulum period.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The calculation in Pendulum Period applies the standard Science method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with length of 1, gravity of 9.81, pendulum period returns <strong>Period: 2.006 s</strong>. The same run reports Frequency: 0.498 Hz | Small-angle approximation (±15°).</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: T = 2π√(L/g)</li><li>L/g = 1/9.81 = 0.1019</li><li>√ = 0.3193</li><li>T = 2π × 0.3193 = 2.006 s</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Read the period first, then the steps: together they show not just the value but why that value follows from your inputs in Pendulum Period.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Students, planners, and professionals use pendulum period for pendulum period planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the period, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic pendulum period mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Pendulum Period twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the period is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Pendulum Period assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the period proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Pendulum Period puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Pendulum Period calculate?","a":"Use Pendulum Period when the period needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Science method and shows the working, not just the answer. Because the working is visible: Pendulum Period shows each operation behind the period in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the period calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: t = 2π√(l/g), then l/g = 1/9.81 = 0.1019. The engine behind Pendulum Period evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the period you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Pendulum Period?","a":"The Length and Gravity that pendulum period asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the period instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Pendulum Period mean?","a":"The main number pendulum period returns is the period for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Pendulum Period covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the Pendulum Period most useful?","a":"Pendulum Period fits planning and checking: pendulum period planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the period, or any moment when the period needs to be right the first time. If the period looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number in Pendulum Period departed from expectation."}]},
