  'password-entropy': {"title":"Password Entropy Calculator: Password Length","metaDesc":"Free Password Entropy Calculator: get the entropy instantly, with every step of the math shown. Nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/utilities/password-entropy","cat":"utilities","catName":"Utilities","lsi":["password entropy calculator bits strength character pool brute force crack time","password entropy formula","how to calculate password entropy","entropy formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Password Entropy Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator works out the entropy from the Password Length and Character Pool Size, following standard Utilities conventions — the page defaults produce a entropy of 71.5 bits.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Password Length and Character Pool Size.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the entropy, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Utilities formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (password length of 12, character pool size of 62), password entropy calculator returns a entropy of 71.5 bits. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Password Entropy Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator computes the entropy directly from your inputs — the Password Length and Character Pool Size feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Password Entropy Calculator works</h2>\n<p>This page is a working password entropy calculator: enter your values, read the entropy, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived.</p>\n<h2>Using the Password Entropy Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Password Length</strong> — one of the values password entropy calculator builds the calculation from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Character Pool Size</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation in password entropy calculator, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Note the entropy. It updates as you type, and the worked steps below it in password entropy calculator make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>Adjust and re-run. Change one input at a time to see how sensitive the entropy is to it — the fastest way to understand what password entropy calculator is doing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rounding in Password Entropy Calculator follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the entropy is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with password length of 12, character pool size of 62, password entropy calculator returns <strong>Entropy: 71.5 bits</strong>. The same run reports Pool^length = 62^12 | Median crack time at 10¹² guesses/s: 51 years — reasonable | Assumes truly random characters;.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: entropy = length × log₂(pool size)</li><li>log₂(62) = 5.9542</li><li>bits = 12 × 5.9542 = 71.5</li><li>Each extra character multiplies search space by the pool size — length beats complexity</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The entropy is the headline answer; the supporting figures beneath it and the step list in Password Entropy Calculator give the surrounding context needed to judge it.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator fits planning and checking: password entropy planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the entropy, or any moment when the entropy needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Password Entropy Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the entropy. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Bookmark password entropy calculator — after the first visit it works offline, so the entropy is one tap away even without a connection.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the entropy proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because Password Entropy Calculator works the same on phone and desktop, keeps working offline after the first visit, and never asks for sign-up.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Password Entropy Calculator calculate?","a":"Password Entropy Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified entropy — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because it is fast and private — Password Entropy Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed."},{"q":"How is the entropy calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: entropy = length × log₂(pool size), then log₂(62) = 5.9542. Password Entropy Calculator substitutes the Password Length and Character Pool Size into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the entropy rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Password Entropy Calculator?","a":"The Password Length and Character Pool Size that password entropy calculator asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the entropy instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Password Entropy Calculator mean?","a":"The main number password entropy calculator returns is the entropy for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Password Entropy Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the Password Entropy Calculator most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Password Entropy Calculator include password entropy planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the entropy — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. The result is shareable — the link carries your inputs, so a colleague can open the same calculation in Password Entropy Calculator without retyping it."}]},
